WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.002
- Do you have those shoes
in your closet right now?

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:02.502
- I might.

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.128
Lemme see.

00:00:03.128 --> 00:00:03.628
- Yes.

00:00:03.628 --> 00:00:05.505
- I definitely have
a shoe storage.

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:06.339
It's a problem.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:07.799
I don't have that.

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:09.259
But I do have a pair of these.

00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:10.176
These are some of my—

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:10.677
- Oh!

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:11.302
- My faves.

00:00:11.302 --> 00:00:12.178
- Yes!

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:13.596
Did we just become best friends?

00:00:13.596 --> 00:00:14.597
- I think we just did.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:28.445
- Welcome to “Full-Bodied,” where,
as the wine gets darker,

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:29.779
the questions get deeper.

00:00:29.779 --> 00:00:33.199
We are here with one of the best
basketball players of all time.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:36.036
Absolute Hall of Famer
whenever she retires.

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:38.580
The style icon, the
sneaker god and, to me, one

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.124
of the coolest people I've had
the pleasure of working with:

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:44.169
Sue Bird, the one, the only.
[Cork popping]
- Ahhh.

00:00:44.169 --> 00:00:47.505
- We're here at Résonance, one
of my favorite vineyards,

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:51.176
top five most beautiful
vineyards in Oregon.

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:53.303
And they make wines
that match the view.

00:00:53.303 --> 00:00:54.179
First of all, cheers.

00:00:54.179 --> 00:00:55.305
Thank you for doing this.

00:00:55.305 --> 00:00:57.057
I wanted to bring
this bottle to you

00:00:57.057 --> 00:01:01.102
because it is from North
Fork of Long Island, which

00:01:01.102 --> 00:01:01.728
is your hood.

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:04.522
And, to be honest, I don't really
drink a lot of sauvignon blanc.

00:01:04.522 --> 00:01:05.565
But I like this.

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:06.232
- Yeah, it's good.

00:01:06.232 --> 00:01:07.317
- Right? I would drink this, right?

00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:10.111
It's citrusy. It's
refreshing. It's

00:01:10.111 --> 00:01:11.988
like, if I was by the
pool, and it was hot,

00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:13.239
I'd be like, “All right,” right?

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:14.991
It's easy on the palate.

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:18.828
I'm just curious: What is in
the water in Long Island that

00:01:18.828 --> 00:01:21.706
made you as badass as you are?

00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:23.124
- I think it's one
of those things,

00:01:23.124 --> 00:01:26.044
like, if I had an
exact answer for you,

00:01:26.044 --> 00:01:27.545
I'd be, like, bottling it up.

00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:28.922
I'd be putting it in a bottle.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:33.093
I would get a fancy oxygen
thing, and I would sell it.

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:36.221
No, I think all the
winning is just about—

00:01:36.221 --> 00:01:38.515
yes, you have to be, obviously,
talented and skilled

00:01:38.515 --> 00:01:40.391
and all these things to
get to a certain level.

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:42.227
But I really—it's
gonna sound so corny,

00:01:42.227 --> 00:01:46.439
but I really have always put the
team's success ahead of mine.

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:49.275
And I found that all the
success I could ever want

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.401
comes when we win.

00:01:50.401 --> 00:01:52.278
And now, I'm at a
place in my career,

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.197
partly because of my
age and my experience,

00:01:54.197 --> 00:01:56.449
where I can really
help other people.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:57.784
I can really help my teammates.

00:01:57.784 --> 00:01:59.494
And I can really help
them be their best.

00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:01.204
And then I just ride
them, to be honest.

00:02:01.204 --> 00:02:02.705
I'm like, “Take me
to the motherland.

00:02:02.705 --> 00:02:03.498
Let's go!”

00:02:03.498 --> 00:02:05.500
So I've been lucky to
have really good teammates

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.793
along the way too.

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:10.505
- You have so many championships
and so many Olympic gold

00:02:10.505 --> 00:02:11.047
medals.

00:02:11.047 --> 00:02:14.342
Which one was the one
where you were like, “OK,

00:02:14.342 --> 00:02:16.219
I have to dig deep”?

00:02:16.219 --> 00:02:20.223
- It's probably—the one that's
freshest is we won in 2018.

00:02:20.223 --> 00:02:23.226
And it's definitely Seattle,
because in the WNBA,

00:02:23.226 --> 00:02:26.104
like, similar to the NBA, it's like,
it’s hard to win in this league.

00:02:26.104 --> 00:02:27.981
Like, some of it's luck.

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:32.235
And the competition is so close
that in a series, usually

00:02:32.235 --> 00:02:33.111
the best team wins.

00:02:33.111 --> 00:02:34.737
But, like, you never know.

00:02:34.737 --> 00:02:37.323
- [Announcer:] Bird connects!

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:38.658
It's Sue Bird's building!

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.953
- So in 2018, this is, like,
probably, to be honest, like,

00:02:41.953 --> 00:02:43.913
the best moment of my career.

00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:44.706
Like, no joke.

00:02:44.706 --> 00:02:47.417
For it to happen when I was
38 is, like, the crazy part.

00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:49.752
But we played
Phoenix in the semis.

00:02:49.752 --> 00:02:51.880
It was a five-game
series, and it was Game 5.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.133
I ended up scoring, like, 14
or so points in the final five

00:02:55.133 --> 00:02:55.758
or six minutes.

00:02:55.758 --> 00:02:56.384
- [Announcer:] Bird heaves!

00:02:56.384 --> 00:02:56.885
- [Bird:] And it—

00:02:56.885 --> 00:02:57.218
- [Frye:] Dang!

00:02:57.218 --> 00:02:59.637
- Yeah, it was, like, a close game, and
it kinda sealed the game.

00:02:59.637 --> 00:03:00.638
- [Announcer:] Sue Bird!

00:03:00.638 --> 00:03:01.848
It is her night!

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:04.893
- So that was the one where, like,
you talk about digging deep—

00:03:04.893 --> 00:03:05.685
- Yeah.
- There was drama.

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:06.728
You know, it was Game 5.

00:03:06.728 --> 00:03:09.397
We've just, like, you know, went to war
with them for four games.

00:03:09.397 --> 00:03:10.732
And now this is the fifth game.

00:03:10.732 --> 00:03:13.401
Diana Taurasi has never
lost, literally never lost

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:14.736
in an elimination game.

00:03:14.736 --> 00:03:16.863
So it's like, all this
stuff is swirling.

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:18.698
I had broken my nose
the game before.

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:20.533
There was just, like,
a lot going on.

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:23.286
So that one, that
one sticks out.

00:03:23.286 --> 00:03:26.998
- Just seeing the pedigree of
basketball that you have,

00:03:26.998 --> 00:03:28.291
right, and the winningest—

00:03:28.291 --> 00:03:30.251
like, I respect winning
more than anything else

00:03:30.251 --> 00:03:32.170
because I understand
how hard it is.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.005
We've both seen
players and people

00:03:34.005 --> 00:03:37.717
come where you go, “You have
everything. Everything!”

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:40.178
Like, “You can shoot. You
can jump. You're smart.”

00:03:40.178 --> 00:03:44.140
And it's just like their
motivation for being great

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.894
is, like, an outboard motor on
a big yacht of talent, right?

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:48.603
- Yeah. [Laughing]

00:03:48.603 --> 00:03:51.231
- This second one, it
is a white Burgundy.

00:03:51.231 --> 00:03:54.776
And so this is a Premier
cru, which is, like,

00:03:54.776 --> 00:03:56.736
higher level of wine.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:04:00.865
And to be from Burgundy,
right, is to be elite

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:02.659
and to be the best.

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:05.954
And why it's elite is
not only where it's from,

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:08.706
but how they make it and
how they're more hands-off.

00:04:08.706 --> 00:04:10.416
And they allow their wine—

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:12.710
and the simplicities
of it take over.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.880
There's only a few, couple of
people that can do Burgundies.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.050
- Listen, if you want greatness,
you gotta work for it, OK?

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:19.759
[Cork popping]
- Oh, there we go.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:20.885
That's the best
sound in the world.

00:04:20.885 --> 00:04:21.219
- Got it.

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:22.595
- So while you're pouring that—

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:23.513
- Whew, that was close.

00:04:23.513 --> 00:04:25.640
- I would like to
know two things.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.600
No. 1, who
are the top, let's

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.479
say, three to five players
you've ever played against?

00:04:31.479 --> 00:04:34.274
And then who are your
top three to five players

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:36.025
you love watching?

00:04:36.025 --> 00:04:37.360
- OK.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:37.860
Cheers.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.696
- Cheers.

00:04:39.696 --> 00:04:41.823
Now, you taste the difference
between the other one

00:04:41.823 --> 00:04:42.532
and this one?

00:04:42.532 --> 00:04:43.283
- Yeah, definitely.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:44.867
If it was gross,
I would tell you.

00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:46.119
- Yeah, please, please tell me.
- They’re really good.

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:46.661
- If it's gross—

00:04:46.661 --> 00:04:47.161
- They’re really good.

00:04:47.161 --> 00:04:48.037
- Please tell me.

00:04:48.037 --> 00:04:50.498
Then it's somebody else's fault.

00:04:50.498 --> 00:04:52.125
- Best players I’ve played against—

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:53.584
Diana Taurasi, for sure.

00:04:53.584 --> 00:04:54.085
- OK.

00:04:54.085 --> 00:04:54.711
- Lauren Jackson.

00:04:54.711 --> 00:04:55.211
- OK.

00:04:55.211 --> 00:04:56.129
- Breanna Stewart.

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:57.255
I'm definitely biased.

00:04:57.255 --> 00:04:59.674
Those three are my teammates.

00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:00.174
- Yeah.

00:05:00.174 --> 00:05:03.052
- So I had front-row
seats to the greatness.

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:05.596
The next two, I would go...

00:05:05.596 --> 00:05:06.097
dang.

00:05:06.097 --> 00:05:09.726
So I'm gonna pick guards,
because, playing against.

00:05:09.726 --> 00:05:13.396
So every time I got
switched in some capacity

00:05:13.396 --> 00:05:17.483
on to Sheryl Swoopes, lowkey,
I was like, “Ohhh, fuck.”

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:17.984
- Oh, yeah.

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:19.068
- Can we curse on this?

00:05:19.068 --> 00:05:20.653
- You were like, “Bring that help,” right?
- Yeah.

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:22.155
- You were like, “What
side? What side?”

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:23.323
- I was like, “Ohhh, crap.”

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:25.199
And then actually,
the fifth player,

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:27.744
I could go Lindsay Whalen,
give some love to another point

00:05:27.744 --> 00:05:28.328
guard.
- Yep.

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:30.580
- But I'm actually gonna go
Cynthia Cooper, just because,

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:30.872
again—

00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:31.372
- Wow.

00:05:31.372 --> 00:05:32.999
- She only played
in the league when

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:35.585
I was in the league for, like, 10 games.
- Right.

00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:38.171
- She had retired, and she
came back for this one year.

00:05:38.171 --> 00:05:40.131
It happened to be my
second year in the league.

00:05:40.131 --> 00:05:42.550
And she only got to, like—
I don't remember exactly—

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:43.384
10, 15 games.

00:05:43.384 --> 00:05:45.887
But I was one of the, we were one
of the games that we played.

00:05:45.887 --> 00:05:49.640
And so it was, it was amazing to play
against a legend, you know?

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.142
She had started the league.

00:05:51.142 --> 00:05:52.977
Raise the roof,
that was her thing.

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:54.520
- What about on the men's side?

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.813
- Stephon Marbury.

00:05:55.813 --> 00:05:56.314
- Yeah, Steph.

00:05:56.314 --> 00:05:58.441
- He was exciting to watch.

00:05:58.441 --> 00:06:00.360
- He was exciting
to be a teammate.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.361
- Yeah.

00:06:01.361 --> 00:06:02.862
Jimmer Fredette. Does he count?

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:03.821
- Jimmer!

00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:05.365
Yes!

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:06.949
- I mean, he's fun to
watch cuz he's

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:08.368
the kinda guy
where you're like,

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:10.495
“Oh, this guy's been
dropping 40 in college?”

00:06:10.495 --> 00:06:11.871
Like, “Let's see
what he's about.”

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:12.997
That kind of—you know?

00:06:13.289 --> 00:06:14.874
- We're gonna take
a hard left, OK?
- OK.

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:17.752
- So when did you become
addicted to sneakers?

00:06:17.752 --> 00:06:19.212
This is actually
an intervention.

00:06:19.212 --> 00:06:20.630
- I feel like the
world should know

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:22.590
I do have it under control.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.259
I'm acknowledging
the addiction, but—

00:06:25.259 --> 00:06:27.887
no, I bet it's a similar story.

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:29.347
I'm sure you have
a ton of sneakers

00:06:29.347 --> 00:06:31.224
just from, like,
being an athlete.

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:32.642
You just get a bunch of stuff.

00:06:32.642 --> 00:06:34.936
I, like, literally woke up one day,
and I was like, “Oh, wow. I

00:06:34.936 --> 00:06:35.686
have a collection.”

00:06:35.686 --> 00:06:40.191
But it wasn’t—I wasn't, like, going out of my
way to, like, stand on the line

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:40.900
somewhere.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:41.401
- Right, right, right.

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:43.277
- I wasn't hitting my
Nike rep constantly.

00:06:43.277 --> 00:06:44.987
It was just like,
they've done a good job

00:06:44.987 --> 00:06:46.364
of getting us the good stuff.

00:06:46.364 --> 00:06:48.449
And now, it's, like, kind
of part of our culture.

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:49.575
- Right.
- It wasn't when
you think about it.

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:51.160
It wasn't, especially
for a woman.

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:53.037
It wasn't, like, five years ago.

00:06:53.037 --> 00:06:55.790
So I always joke, I've
been, like, waiting for the day—

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:56.290
- Yeah.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:59.168
- For sneakers to be
appropriate to wear to, like,

00:06:59.168 --> 00:07:00.044
a black-tie event.

00:07:00.044 --> 00:07:01.337
I've been waiting for this day.

00:07:01.337 --> 00:07:05.049
- See, my kick game
is lowkey only good

00:07:05.049 --> 00:07:07.218
because I was in the
league so long, all of Nike

00:07:07.218 --> 00:07:07.927
felt bad for me.

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:10.513
And when I moved to Portland, I
know where they all live, so I

00:07:10.513 --> 00:07:12.098
bug 'em. Shoutout, Ted.

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:12.723
And so, you know, for me—

00:07:12.723 --> 00:07:14.183
- You're at the
employee store like—

00:07:14.183 --> 00:07:15.435
- I'm always like, “Hey”—yeah, oh,

00:07:15.435 --> 00:07:17.395
they’re like,
“Channing, dammit!

00:07:17.395 --> 00:07:18.688
Hide the 18s.”

00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:22.024
So there's only so many cool
shoes you can get in a size 18.

00:07:22.024 --> 00:07:23.860
So, you know, I've been kinda hampered.

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.820
Wait till you see the
kicks I got on right now.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:27.071
Could you guess what they are?

00:07:27.071 --> 00:07:29.282
- Maybe not-so-fresh
pair of Air Force 1s.

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:30.241
That would be my guess.

00:07:30.241 --> 00:07:31.242
- No.

00:07:31.242 --> 00:07:32.869
No, here, watch these bad boys.

00:07:32.869 --> 00:07:36.497
Look, these are a fresh
pair of Croc Air Force 1s.

00:07:36.497 --> 00:07:39.167
- [Laughing] Oh, Croc—hey, got
the UGG slippers.

00:07:39.167 --> 00:07:39.709
- Do you?

00:07:39.709 --> 00:07:40.376
OK!

00:07:40.376 --> 00:07:43.296
Listen, sometimes, you gotta
let the big dogs breathe now.

00:07:43.296 --> 00:07:43.796
Look—

00:07:43.796 --> 00:07:44.922
- Yeah, cozy. Gotta be cozy.

00:07:44.922 --> 00:07:47.091
- What is it about
sneakers that you love?

00:07:47.091 --> 00:07:50.219
And what is it, like, about your
favorite pair that you love?

00:07:50.219 --> 00:07:51.304
Is it the exclusivity?

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:52.346
Is it the colorway?

00:07:52.346 --> 00:07:53.347
Is it the story?

00:07:53.347 --> 00:07:53.890
What is that?

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.393
- So, like, my favorite
pairs are more the story.

00:07:57.393 --> 00:07:59.187
One of my all-time
favorite pairs,

00:07:59.187 --> 00:08:01.439
and I have, like, my originals—

00:08:01.439 --> 00:08:02.106
- Right.

00:08:02.106 --> 00:08:04.317
- Well, actually, no,
that's not entirely true.

00:08:04.317 --> 00:08:06.777
I don't have, like,
the O-O-OG version.

00:08:06.777 --> 00:08:08.905
I have, like, when it
came out the second time.

00:08:08.905 --> 00:08:10.948
It's since come out, like,
three and four times.

00:08:10.948 --> 00:08:13.034
The Andre Agassis.
They're like the—

00:08:13.034 --> 00:08:13.534
- Yes!

00:08:13.534 --> 00:08:14.619
Oh, the tennis joints!

00:08:14.619 --> 00:08:17.246
- Air [Tech] Challenges. Yeah,
with the pink on the side.

00:08:17.246 --> 00:08:19.665
I remember getting those
in, like, elementary school.

00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:22.502
When they came out again and
I could get them as an adult,

00:08:22.502 --> 00:08:24.086
those are the same
ones I still have.

00:08:24.086 --> 00:08:26.797
And so, like, those are the ones
that I hold on to tight.

00:08:26.797 --> 00:08:29.342
- Growing up in Arizona, like, remember
when Charles Barkley came out

00:08:29.342 --> 00:08:32.345
with the CB 34s with
the first air bubble?

00:08:32.345 --> 00:08:34.805
- Those are my favorite—
those are, like, one of my first

00:08:34.805 --> 00:08:36.474
and one of my favorite
basketball shoes.

00:08:36.474 --> 00:08:37.266
- Yes!

00:08:37.266 --> 00:08:37.892
- Like, obsessed.

00:08:37.892 --> 00:08:39.352
- So I could not—

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:40.895
I was crying to
my dad. Like, “Dad,

00:08:40.895 --> 00:08:43.314
please let me get these shoes.”

00:08:43.314 --> 00:08:44.148
He was like, “Dude.”

00:08:44.148 --> 00:08:47.485
So in sixth grade, I
was 6'2" wearing, like—

00:08:47.485 --> 00:08:47.985
- Damn.

00:08:47.985 --> 00:08:49.820
- You know—how old are
you in sixth grade, 12?

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.740
Wearing a size 13, 14
shoe in Chuck Taylors.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:53.282
- That's crazy.

00:08:53.282 --> 00:08:54.742
- But you know Chucks
run big, right?

00:08:54.742 --> 00:08:55.243
- Yeah.

00:08:55.243 --> 00:08:58.621
- So, like, I was wearing a 14
because my dad was like, “Well,

00:08:58.621 --> 00:08:59.872
you're gonna grow into 'em.”
- “You’re gonna grow into ‘em.”

00:08:59.872 --> 00:09:00.498
- So imagine—

00:09:00.498 --> 00:09:00.998
- Aw.

00:09:00.998 --> 00:09:02.208
- Yeah!

00:09:02.208 --> 00:09:03.292
- Damn.

00:09:03.292 --> 00:09:06.087
- I was getting roasted—

00:09:06.087 --> 00:09:06.587
- Yeah, I bet.

00:09:06.587 --> 00:09:10.925
- Roasted at school back in
the '90s, with my big clippy

00:09:10.925 --> 00:09:12.385
cloppy flippers.

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:14.345
But, man, it's just like,
sneakers are evolving.

00:09:14.345 --> 00:09:16.347
And they're awesome, and
they have these stories.

00:09:16.347 --> 00:09:19.183
But just those original shoes
that you fell in love with

00:09:19.183 --> 00:09:20.434
are unreal.

00:09:20.434 --> 00:09:21.060
- Yeah.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.897
So it's funny you say
that, because my favorite,

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:27.692
by far, like, all-time
favorite basketball shoe

00:09:27.692 --> 00:09:29.735
is the original Uptempos.

00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:30.236
Like—

00:09:30.236 --> 00:09:30.820
- Oh!

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.407
- And about, I dunno,
a year ago or so,

00:09:34.407 --> 00:09:36.284
I was like, “Let me
work out in these.”

00:09:36.284 --> 00:09:38.035
Like, these are some
of my favorite shoes.

00:09:38.035 --> 00:09:39.203
Like, lemme go work out.

00:09:39.203 --> 00:09:40.955
Oh, I had to take ‘em
off in, like, 20 minutes.

00:09:40.955 --> 00:09:42.331
I was like, “These
are unwearable.”

00:09:42.331 --> 00:09:45.418
I feel like our feet
are now a little, a little snobby.

00:09:45.418 --> 00:09:45.960
We're very—

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.379
- Do you have those shoes
in your closet right now?

00:09:48.379 --> 00:09:48.879
- I might.

00:09:48.879 --> 00:09:49.505
Lemme see.

00:09:49.505 --> 00:09:51.173
- Yes.

00:09:51.173 --> 00:09:51.924
- I don't know.

00:09:51.924 --> 00:09:54.010
Yeah, I definitely
have a shoe storage.

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:55.094
It's a problem.

00:09:55.094 --> 00:09:56.554
- Yeah, well, I got wine storage—
- I don’t know if I have ‘em here.

00:09:56.554 --> 00:09:58.723
- So it's a problem, too.

00:09:58.723 --> 00:10:00.433
- Let me see what I can
break out, though.

00:10:00.433 --> 00:10:04.270
All right, I don't, I don’t have that.
But I do have a pair of these.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:05.187
These are some of my—

00:10:05.187 --> 00:10:05.688
- Oh!

00:10:05.688 --> 00:10:06.188
- My faves.

00:10:06.188 --> 00:10:07.106
- Yes!

00:10:07.106 --> 00:10:09.942
I hooped in those my
senior year in college.

00:10:09.942 --> 00:10:11.986
This is before I tell—
so nobody tell Bron.

00:10:11.986 --> 00:10:13.404
Nobody show Bron.

00:10:13.404 --> 00:10:17.074
But I literally loved
playing in those.

00:10:17.074 --> 00:10:19.744
I thought I could jump—it
actually helped me get drafted.

00:10:19.744 --> 00:10:21.662
You know, you gotta fake
it to make it sometimes.

00:10:21.662 --> 00:10:22.830
- Yep. They're so comfortable

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.915
- Any shoe with a strap is perfect.

00:10:24.915 --> 00:10:26.417
- Same!
- Any shoe with a strap is perfect.

00:10:26.417 --> 00:10:26.917
- Same.

00:10:26.917 --> 00:10:29.003
- Oh, my goodness. Did we
just become best friends?

00:10:29.003 --> 00:10:30.546
- I think we just did.

00:10:30.546 --> 00:10:32.757
- We can open up these
red bottles now.

00:10:32.757 --> 00:10:34.800
And so why we picked
this—obviously

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.053
Ponzi has been around
Portland and obviously

00:10:37.053 --> 00:10:39.847
Willamette Valley
for a long time.

00:10:39.847 --> 00:10:42.808
And the winery owners are
women, powerful women.

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:43.559
- Yeah, I saw that.

00:10:43.559 --> 00:10:45.394
- Wonderful, awesome,
awesome people.

00:10:45.394 --> 00:10:47.855
And so they are just
doing things together

00:10:47.855 --> 00:10:49.732
where they are
supporting human rights

00:10:49.732 --> 00:10:52.568
and making sure that everyone
is gonna be treated equally.

00:10:52.568 --> 00:10:56.906
I will say this: You
have done so much for not

00:10:56.906 --> 00:11:00.326
only inside the WNBA
and using your platform,

00:11:00.326 --> 00:11:02.036
but just all walks of life.

00:11:02.036 --> 00:11:03.329
I would consider you a leader.

00:11:03.329 --> 00:11:06.374
I would consider you one
of the forefront of that.

00:11:06.374 --> 00:11:08.000
And that is a big
responsibility.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:09.794
So how do you take that?

00:11:09.794 --> 00:11:11.003
How did that come about?

00:11:11.003 --> 00:11:13.089
- I have this, like, interesting
career just because I've

00:11:13.089 --> 00:11:14.715
been playing for so long.

00:11:14.715 --> 00:11:17.343
So when I entered the league,
the league was different.

00:11:17.343 --> 00:11:20.096
The world was different. Society
was different. All that stuff.

00:11:20.096 --> 00:11:20.596
- Oh, yeah.

00:11:20.596 --> 00:11:22.264
- So when I first got
into the league,

00:11:22.264 --> 00:11:24.058
and really, most of
my career—like,

00:11:24.058 --> 00:11:28.020
10-plus years—it just—
we had this vibe of like,

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:29.605
“Be thankful that
you can even play.”

00:11:29.605 --> 00:11:31.357
- Right.
- You know, “Don't really push the limits.

00:11:31.357 --> 00:11:32.692
Don't speak on these things.”

00:11:32.692 --> 00:11:34.568
It wasn't that somebody
told me not to do it.

00:11:34.568 --> 00:11:35.653
It was just, like, a vibe.

00:11:35.653 --> 00:11:37.613
I kind of, like, understood
what was happening.

00:11:37.613 --> 00:11:38.280
And so I didn't.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.074
- Very oppressive vibe?

00:11:40.074 --> 00:11:42.118
- Yeah.

00:11:42.118 --> 00:11:43.369
- You can say that.

00:11:43.369 --> 00:11:45.538
- I think a combination
of things—one,

00:11:45.538 --> 00:11:47.665
just getting older,
more experience,

00:11:47.665 --> 00:11:50.501
getting more confident
in, like, who you are.

00:11:50.501 --> 00:11:53.879
A lot of that has to do
with when I came out.

00:11:53.879 --> 00:11:56.132
That's a big part of, like,
saying who you are.
- Right.

00:11:56.132 --> 00:11:59.009
- It just, it gives you this
whole other confidence.

00:11:59.009 --> 00:12:02.221
So some of it's just, like,
natural progression of life.

00:12:02.221 --> 00:12:03.514
Some of it is meeting Megan.

00:12:03.514 --> 00:12:07.768
And I think what you see now is
just someone who feels like—

00:12:07.768 --> 00:12:10.563
I don't regret the early parts
of my career. In some ways,

00:12:10.563 --> 00:12:11.856
I don't know that I was ready.

00:12:11.856 --> 00:12:14.024
There are, you know, things
that need to be said.

00:12:14.024 --> 00:12:17.737
And I have a lot of the
experience that I can draw on.

00:12:17.737 --> 00:12:19.905
And I just don't wanna
miss another moment, right?

00:12:19.905 --> 00:12:22.241
Like, I feel like I missed a lot
of opportunities in the past,

00:12:22.241 --> 00:12:23.993
so I just don't wanna
miss them anymore.

00:12:23.993 --> 00:12:26.954
And that's kind of, I think,
what you see now from me.

00:12:26.954 --> 00:12:30.750
- What is something that the
men can do to uplift you guys,

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.835
and to, like, be hand in hand?

00:12:32.835 --> 00:12:34.587
- These are, like, kind of
tough questions.

00:12:34.587 --> 00:12:36.213
I don't know that I
have exact answers.

00:12:36.213 --> 00:12:38.716
I would say—I do think
language is important.

00:12:38.716 --> 00:12:41.635
It's not just how we're
empowering our daughters,

00:12:41.635 --> 00:12:42.303
which we should.
- Yeah.

00:12:42.303 --> 00:12:44.430
- It's not just how we're
encouraging our daughters,

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:45.556
which we should.

00:12:45.556 --> 00:12:47.725
It's also what we're
teaching our sons.
- Right, right.

00:12:47.725 --> 00:12:50.019
- Because those are gonna
be the next generation.

00:12:50.019 --> 00:12:52.563
It's like, you have to come from
a place of willing to learn

00:12:52.563 --> 00:12:57.443
and willing to, like, maybe
at times, in that growth

00:12:57.443 --> 00:12:58.194
and in that—

00:12:58.194 --> 00:12:59.069
- Evolution?

00:12:59.069 --> 00:13:01.155
- Yeah, evolution, and, like,
the more knowledge

00:13:01.155 --> 00:13:04.158
you gain. Also,
kind of understanding

00:13:04.158 --> 00:13:06.285
maybe the privilege
you've had, or maybe

00:13:06.285 --> 00:13:09.497
the things you've said in the
past that weren't, you know, on the up

00:13:09.497 --> 00:13:12.374
and up and just kind of, like,
acknowledging that. Not getting

00:13:12.374 --> 00:13:14.877
defensive, but just
acknowledging it

00:13:14.877 --> 00:13:17.505
and then, like, fixing
it and moving on.

00:13:17.505 --> 00:13:20.508
It's kind of like when you
tell a white person, “You

00:13:20.508 --> 00:13:24.178
had privilege,” for
some reason, it's like

00:13:24.178 --> 00:13:27.681
this reaction of like,
“No, I worked hard,”

00:13:27.681 --> 00:13:29.141
you know what I mean?

00:13:29.141 --> 00:13:29.725
- Yes.

00:13:29.725 --> 00:13:30.226
- And it's like, “Yeah, no—”

00:13:30.226 --> 00:13:30.267
- Oh, yeah.

00:13:30.267 --> 00:13:31.602
I live in Portland, trust me.

00:13:31.602 --> 00:13:32.102
- Yeah.

00:13:32.102 --> 00:13:35.397
[Laughing] But it's like,
“Yeah, you did work hard.

00:13:35.397 --> 00:13:36.816
I didn't say you
didn't work hard.

00:13:36.816 --> 00:13:38.234
But you had—you
have privilege.

00:13:38.234 --> 00:13:39.693
Like, you had a head start.”
- Yeah, yeah.

00:13:39.693 --> 00:13:41.946
- It's a similar—it's similar.

00:13:41.946 --> 00:13:42.738
It's not the same.

00:13:42.738 --> 00:13:46.116
I'm not comparing in that
way, but, like, the lesson

00:13:46.116 --> 00:13:46.826
is similar.

00:13:46.826 --> 00:13:50.371
And then I think what you said
about the media is so crucial.

00:13:50.371 --> 00:13:50.871
- Yeah.

00:13:50.871 --> 00:13:53.457
- Giving women the airtime
and not just, like,

00:13:53.457 --> 00:13:54.708
showing the highlight.

00:13:54.708 --> 00:13:57.545
But I hope we get to a
point in our storytelling

00:13:57.545 --> 00:13:59.255
where we're, like,
acknowledging the storylines

00:13:59.255 --> 00:14:00.256
in women's sports.
- Yeah.

00:14:00.256 --> 00:14:02.842
- And I think for women's sports,
we never tap into those,

00:14:02.842 --> 00:14:06.095
so people never get invested the
way they are in men's sports.

00:14:06.095 --> 00:14:08.013
It's like a small part
of it, but it matters.

00:14:08.013 --> 00:14:11.642
- On that, on the youth of
the women of the WNBA,

00:14:11.642 --> 00:14:14.979
where do you see it evolving in
the next five years, 10 years,

00:14:14.979 --> 00:14:15.896
20 years?

00:14:15.896 --> 00:14:18.858
Are you gonna be—and I'm
asking you personally, what is

00:14:18.858 --> 00:14:19.608
that next goal?

00:14:19.608 --> 00:14:20.693
Would you wanna be an owner?

00:14:20.693 --> 00:14:21.902
Would you wanna be a coach?

00:14:21.902 --> 00:14:23.863
- I don't know. That's
the short answer.

00:14:23.863 --> 00:14:24.363
But—

00:14:24.363 --> 00:14:26.031
- That’s aight.

00:14:26.031 --> 00:14:28.450
- It's starting to become
a little more clear.

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:29.869
I do know that I wanna be—

00:14:29.869 --> 00:14:32.079
I'm always gonna be
involved in women's sports.

00:14:32.079 --> 00:14:33.998
So I'm sure you've
heard of Paige Bueckers.

00:14:33.998 --> 00:14:34.832
She plays at Connecticut.

00:14:34.832 --> 00:14:35.332
- Yeah. Oh, yeah.

00:14:35.332 --> 00:14:36.333
- She's a freshman.

00:14:36.333 --> 00:14:36.834
She's—

00:14:36.834 --> 00:14:38.085
- Good gracious almighty.

00:14:38.085 --> 00:14:39.670
- Yeah, insanely good.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:42.131
Like, you can't deny it.

00:14:42.131 --> 00:14:45.843
The best thing about her
and, like, her generation—

00:14:45.843 --> 00:14:47.094
so there's a bunch of players.

00:14:47.094 --> 00:14:49.930
Azzi Fudd's another
one. Hailey Van Lith.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:51.265
I mentioned Caitlin Clark.

00:14:51.265 --> 00:14:53.350
Like, they have these
followings right now.

00:14:53.350 --> 00:14:55.102
I'm just saying like,
you, Channing Frye,

00:14:55.102 --> 00:14:58.647
a former NBA player who's now
retired, knows her name.

00:14:58.647 --> 00:14:59.148
And your kids—

00:14:59.148 --> 00:14:59.648
- Oh, for sure.

00:14:59.648 --> 00:15:00.941
I've watched a couple of games.

00:15:00.941 --> 00:15:01.984
She's a walking bucket.

00:15:01.984 --> 00:15:02.693
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:15:02.693 --> 00:15:04.570
And so people your
kids' age, or—

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:06.322
there's gonna be a
generation of people

00:15:06.322 --> 00:15:08.115
that grow up with Paige.

00:15:08.115 --> 00:15:12.286
And so when they become
the 24-, 25-year-old person

00:15:12.286 --> 00:15:15.414
who can go out and buy a
ticket, who has a family,

00:15:15.414 --> 00:15:17.875
Paige is gonna be a
household name already.

00:15:17.875 --> 00:15:19.668
Like, it's starting earlier.

00:15:19.668 --> 00:15:22.338
So in five years, I'm
really excited for where

00:15:22.338 --> 00:15:25.132
women's basketball could be
because that era of player,

00:15:25.132 --> 00:15:26.675
they already have a fanbase.

00:15:26.675 --> 00:15:31.055
I had like, the UConn fanbase,
but it wasn't quite the same.

00:15:31.055 --> 00:15:33.432
She's going to take that with
her for the next four years

00:15:33.432 --> 00:15:34.016
and beyond.

00:15:34.016 --> 00:15:36.435
Like I said, I don't know
exactly how it's going to look.

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:36.936
- Right. Right.

00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:40.022
- But I can't imagine I'm not
involved in some way, shape

00:15:40.022 --> 00:15:40.522
or form.

00:15:40.522 --> 00:15:42.816
Like, ownership—I think
I'd be more likely to get

00:15:42.816 --> 00:15:45.736
involved on the ownership side
than I would the coaching side.

00:15:45.736 --> 00:15:47.780
I feel like coaching's
kind of hard.

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:48.489
- Oof.

00:15:48.489 --> 00:15:49.448
No, thank you. No, thank you.

00:15:49.448 --> 00:15:51.283
- And I feel like I
already lived this life.

00:15:51.283 --> 00:15:53.911
I don't know if I want to live
it again. You know what I mean?

00:15:53.911 --> 00:15:55.079
- We have a game, right?

00:15:55.079 --> 00:15:56.330
And this—we'll end on this.

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:56.997
- OK.

00:15:56.997 --> 00:15:58.374
- So since you have nothing
else to do tonight,

00:15:58.374 --> 00:16:00.000
you've got to take
a sip for every time

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.460
you get it wrong, OK?

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:01.961
Here we go.

00:16:01.961 --> 00:16:02.461
- I'm in.

00:16:02.461 --> 00:16:03.003
- Right?

00:16:03.003 --> 00:16:05.089
So it's called Fill
In the Grape, OK?

00:16:05.089 --> 00:16:07.591
So we have five categories
with a question in each.

00:16:07.591 --> 00:16:09.218
You're going to choose
your categories,

00:16:09.218 --> 00:16:10.928
and then you have
to say the answer.

00:16:10.928 --> 00:16:11.428
- OK.

00:16:11.428 --> 00:16:17.267
- So it's Huskies in the W, Kicks,
History Books, WNBA Assists

00:16:17.267 --> 00:16:18.352
or Megan.

00:16:18.352 --> 00:16:19.937
- I'll go Huskies in the W.

00:16:19.937 --> 00:16:23.565
- Name five UConn players who
play in the Western Conference.

00:16:23.565 --> 00:16:26.193
- Breanna Stewart and
Katie Lou Samuelson.

00:16:26.193 --> 00:16:27.236
- OK.

00:16:27.236 --> 00:16:31.115
- Diana Taurasi, Moriah
Jefferson, Crystal Dangerfield.

00:16:31.115 --> 00:16:31.782
Boom!

00:16:31.782 --> 00:16:33.158
- You kicked my butt on that one.

00:16:33.158 --> 00:16:34.743
- History Books sounds
like a wild card,

00:16:34.743 --> 00:16:35.869
so I'll take History Books.

00:16:35.869 --> 00:16:38.080
- Can you name two NBA
players—there's

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:41.625
only two NBA players who,
just like you, have won titles

00:16:41.625 --> 00:16:43.335
in three different decades.

00:16:43.335 --> 00:16:45.212
- I feel like LeBron has to be—

00:16:45.212 --> 00:16:46.130
- Nope.

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:46.880
- No?

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:48.007
- No, uh huh.

00:16:48.007 --> 00:16:48.841
- Tim Duncan?

00:16:48.841 --> 00:16:49.633
- Yeah.

00:16:49.633 --> 00:16:50.759
Now, he is a big man.

00:16:50.759 --> 00:16:52.511
I think he went to Georgia Tech.

00:16:52.511 --> 00:16:53.846
Somebody in my ear, tell me.

00:16:53.846 --> 00:16:54.805
- He went to Georgia Tech?

00:16:54.805 --> 00:16:57.516
- Very outgoing. It
was John Salley.

00:16:57.516 --> 00:16:59.018
- Oh, John Salley.

00:16:59.018 --> 00:16:59.643
The Best Damn—

00:16:59.643 --> 00:17:00.227
- That's a name.

00:17:00.227 --> 00:17:01.895
- The Best Damn Sports Show.

00:17:01.895 --> 00:17:03.355
- Yes!

00:17:03.355 --> 00:17:04.898
- How am I going to mess that up?

00:17:04.898 --> 00:17:06.567
OK, we'll go Kicks.

00:17:06.567 --> 00:17:09.486
- What sneakers did you wear when
you won your first championship

00:17:09.486 --> 00:17:11.196
in 2004?

00:17:11.196 --> 00:17:13.365
- Oh, the 2K4s, Huaraches.

00:17:13.365 --> 00:17:13.866
- I knew.

00:17:13.866 --> 00:17:14.992
I could have said that.

00:17:14.992 --> 00:17:16.577
Everybody was playing in those!

00:17:16.577 --> 00:17:17.244
- Everybody.

00:17:17.244 --> 00:17:21.415
- Before you broke
the record in 2017,

00:17:21.415 --> 00:17:24.501
who was the WNBA
all-time assist leader?

00:17:24.501 --> 00:17:25.461
- Oh, Ticha Penicheiro.

00:17:25.461 --> 00:17:26.295
- Ah, that's so easy.

00:17:26.295 --> 00:17:26.712
Come on, guys!

00:17:26.712 --> 00:17:27.588
- Yeah, these are easy.

00:17:27.588 --> 00:17:29.256
You guys are lobbing them in.

00:17:29.256 --> 00:17:30.257
Give me the Megan one.

00:17:30.257 --> 00:17:31.175
Give me the Megan one.

00:17:31.175 --> 00:17:31.675
- OK.

00:17:31.675 --> 00:17:35.387
In 2019, against what team
or country did Megan convert

00:17:35.387 --> 00:17:39.349
two penalty kicks in round of
16 to help the U.S. secure a win?

00:17:39.349 --> 00:17:42.019
- Ah, see? Umm, France?

00:17:42.019 --> 00:17:43.020
- Here we go, gotcha.

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:44.146
Take a little sippy sippy.

00:17:44.146 --> 00:17:44.646
- Megan!

00:17:44.646 --> 00:17:47.566
- [Laughing]

00:17:47.566 --> 00:17:49.276
- You can phone a friend.

00:17:49.276 --> 00:17:50.486
Ask her. See if she remembers.

00:17:50.486 --> 00:17:52.529
- I think she's—Megan!

00:17:52.529 --> 00:17:53.864
Maybe Sweden?

00:17:53.864 --> 00:17:55.657
- OK, take one more sip.

00:17:55.657 --> 00:17:56.408
- I don't know—

00:17:56.408 --> 00:17:56.909
Germany?

00:17:56.909 --> 00:17:57.868
I have no idea who they played.

00:17:57.868 --> 00:17:59.078
- Ooh, take one more sip.

00:17:59.078 --> 00:18:00.037
You gonna be lit, girl.

00:18:00.037 --> 00:18:00.788
What you doing?

00:18:00.788 --> 00:18:02.164
It's Tuesday!

00:18:02.164 --> 00:18:03.499
Y'all watching The Voice?

00:18:03.499 --> 00:18:04.958
It was Spain.

00:18:04.958 --> 00:18:05.459
- Spain.

00:18:05.459 --> 00:18:05.876
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:18:05.876 --> 00:18:06.585
Aw, I told you.

00:18:06.585 --> 00:18:07.086
Damn it.

00:18:07.086 --> 00:18:08.504
- Yeah, I never would
have gotten it.

00:18:08.504 --> 00:18:09.046
- Oh well.

00:18:09.046 --> 00:18:10.339
Thank you for being a leader.

00:18:10.339 --> 00:18:11.673
Thank you for taking the time.

00:18:11.673 --> 00:18:12.591
Thank you for being awesome.

00:18:12.633 --> 00:18:13.592
- Thank you so much for having me.

00:18:13.759 --> 00:18:14.426
I had a good time.

00:18:14.426 --> 00:18:18.263
- Another episode of “Full-Bodied.”This has been great. Sue Bird.

